Implement a new game called "Smugglers". Make a plan first, then implement.

There is one player who plays as interpol and the rest are smugglers (select interpol randomly).

Players are playing on a board which is composed of a number of planets with connecting routes. Have a fixed board that is used each time but set it up so that there can be multiple game boards in the future that can just be swapped in. For now just have one. Connections should have a length represent this as little dots along it. Ships can move partially between planets and stop on the little dots. Connections may also be between dots. (Nodes of the graph are dots or planets)

The first map should have:
- 15 planets loosely connected
- connections should be around 2-5 long each
- each planet can be one of three different colors
- give each planet a name


Smugglers start with 2 ships each. These are a token on the board and a tile in front of the player. Each turn players can move their ships according to their movement speed (shown on the ship tile) (this is one action). Players can also store goods (cards) in the ship. Generally, smugglers are trying to pick up goods at some planets, load their ships, travel to other planets, and complete contracts to gain credits (points/win condition). Some planets allow players to draw cards and load their cargo, other planets are deposit only. Smugglers start with 5 goods in hand but can only add them to their ships at specific points in time. Smugglers get one action for each of their ships per turn.

There is a face up row of 5 cards next to the board which are the currently available contracts (there is a deck from where these are drawn). Each card indicates a source planet and a destination planet. When a contract is revealed, place a token on the source planet to indicate cargo there to be picked up (a matching token goes on the card). A smuggler that reaches that planet can use an action to draw 5 cards, place any number of cards on their ship (face down) at that location, and discard down to 5. The smuggler then moves the cargo token from the planet to their ship.

When a ship with cargo reaches the destination on one of the contracts, the player may complete the contract without spending an action. The contract awards the smuggler credits for each card in the largest matching set of legal goods (amount per card indicated on the good card), additional +X credits if it matches the good on the contract (X is written on the contract), and additional credits for each illegal good (indicated on the good card).

The good deck should have lots of legal goods with values 1, 2, 3 (space themed). And a handful of ilegal goods with larger point values 7 8 9 (space themed). Contracts should only ask for legal goods for now

Interpol gets a deck of ability cards. They can place barriers to block travel in certain locations. They have patrol ships that they can move to investigate cargo. They have checkpoints that require players to declare the type of cargo that they have. Interpol starts with 5 cards in their deck and draws 3 per turn. Make this dominion style where they can buy more cards for their deck and some cards let them play more cards per turn or draw more cards or duplicate the cards they have played. Some cards should "clog" their deck like barrier or patrol but give them a movable/placeable piece on the board. Make something simple here for now, we will itterate on this more.


